/* ── FWA screen ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* Structure only. Every rule below restates something FWAScreen.DEFAULT_CSS
 * already says, because an app stylesheet always outranks DEFAULT_CSS in
 * Textual -- whatever this block leaves out is not "inherited from the
 * screen", it is silently replaced by whatever this file happens to say for
 * the shared ids (#middle-row, #right-col, #bottom-row, #separator,
 * #title-bar, lines 12-130), which is exactly how TTT and Talismans work.
 *
 * The colour work for this screen lives in the `fwa` Theme
 * (themes/__init__.py), not here: this stylesheet is shared by all ten themes,
 * so it may only name universal variables. Referencing a theme-specific
 * variable such as `$fwa-crown-gold` here would raise a parse error under the
 * other nine.
 */

FWAHeroMetrics {
    height: 7;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* padding is `0 2` and MUST stay `0 2`.
 *
 * The box is `height: 7` with a solid border, i.e. five interior rows, and the
 * hero cards compose four lines: title, big number, second line, coverage
 * badge. `padding: 1 2` -- what TalismansHeroBox above uses, and what an early
 * draft of this block called for -- spends two of those rows on whitespace and
 * clips the fourth line. On the PULL EV card the clipped line is the coverage
 * badge, which would leave a confident-looking EV number with its uncertainty
 * removed: precisely what PRD §3 forbids.
 *
 * tests/screens/test_fwa_screen.py::test_ev_coverage_badge_survives_the_real_stylesheet
 * mounts this file and asserts the badge reaches the compositor, so this is
 * enforced rather than merely documented. Do not "tidy" it to match the
 * Talismans block. (TalismansHeroBox loses its own subtitle this way today --
 * a separate, known defect, deliberately not fixed here.)
 */
FWAHeroBox {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 7;
    border: solid $panel;
    padding: 0 2;
    content-align: center middle;
    text-align: center;
    background: $surface;
    margin: 0 1;
}

FWAOddsBoard {
    width: 3fr;
    padding: 0 1;
}

FWAOddsBoard > .fwa-odds-title {
    color: $text-muted;
}

FWAOddsBoard DataTable {
    height: 1fr;
    background: $background;
    scrollbar-size: 1 1;
}

FWASparkline {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
    content-align: center top;
}

FWASparkline > .fwa-spark-title {
    color: $text-muted;
}

FWASignals {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    content-align: center top;
}

FWASignals > .fwa-signals-title {
    color: $text-muted;
}

/* The feed shares the middle-left slot with FWAOddsBoard, toggled with `c`.
 * `3fr` against #right-col's `2fr` is the odds board's own ratio, so the two
 * views are exactly the same size -- which is why moving the feed out of the
 * bottom row needed no rule change here. Both fill the row height with no
 * explicit `height`; adding one measures identically and would be dead CSS. */
FWAActivityFeed {
    width: 3fr;
    padding: 0 1;
}

FWAActivityFeed > .fwa-feed-title {
    color: $text-muted;
}

FWAActivityFeed RichLog {
    background: $background;
    scrollbar-size: 1 1;
}

/* The two bottom panes, sized by class rather than by content: the column set
 * inside them is responsive, so nothing here may depend on how many columns
 * survive at a given width.
 *
 * `2fr` each is an even split now they have the row to themselves. The
 * activity feed used to take 3fr of this row and left these two ~55 columns
 * apiece at 200 columns, under what either needs for its full column set.
 * Deliberately not rewritten to `1fr`: 2fr+2fr and 1fr+1fr are the same
 * split, and changing the number would imply a behaviour change there is not. */
FWAChaseBoard, FWASettlementTable {
    width: 2fr;
    padding: 0 1;
    content-align: center top;
}

FWAChaseBoard > .fwa-chase-title,
FWASettlementTable > .fwa-settle-title {
    color: $text-muted;
}

FWAChaseBoard DataTable,
FWASettlementTable DataTable {
    height: 1fr;
    background: $background;
    scrollbar-size: 1 1;
}

/* ── Surf screen ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* Structure only, restating SurfScreen.DEFAULT_CSS. An app stylesheet always
 * outranks DEFAULT_CSS in Textual, so anything this block leaves out is not
 * "inherited from the screen" -- it is whatever this file already says for the
 * shared ids (#title-bar, #middle-row, #separator, #bottom-row, lines 12-130).
 * Same reasoning, and same failure mode, as the FWA block above. The two
 * copies are asserted to agree; edit both or neither.
 *
 * The id rules are scoped to `SurfScreen` on purpose. `#hero-row` and
 * `#surf-right-rail` are unused elsewhere today, and an unscoped rule for
 * either would silently become law for the next dashboard that picks the same
 * id -- and the shared `#bottom-row`/`#middle-row` rules above are law for
 * nine other screens, so the surf overrides have to be scoped to beat them
 * here and nowhere else.
 *
 * Colour belongs in a Theme, not here: this stylesheet is shared by all ten
 * themes, so naming a theme-specific variable would raise a parse error under
 * the other nine.
 */

/* `auto`, not a pinned height: SurfHero is seven rows and the row used to be
 * ten, which reserved three blank rows above the feed on every launch.
 *
 * `padding: 0 0` is load-bearing -- vertical padding here clips the hero
 * boxes' bottom border off the screen (the FWA hero-clipping bug), and with
 * `height: auto` it would also silently pad the whole row back out. */
SurfScreen #hero-row {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The hero owns the whole top row: four boxes across the full width get ~26
 * content columns each at 139 terminal columns, which reaches the hero's
 * `full` tier. At the old `3fr` half they got 13 and the `full` tier needed
 * ~220 columns -- more than any terminal anyone owns. */
SurfHero {
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The only `1fr` row on the screen, so every row a taller terminal adds is
 * handed to the feed and the activity panel instead of being stranded at the
 * bottom. */
SurfScreen #middle-row {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0 0;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The middle row's left slot. `7fr` against the rail's `6fr`, the same seam
 * the bottom row uses.
 *
 * The seam is measured, not chosen. The left column's binding panel is the
 * feed (the columns it needs before it starts breaking a post); the right
 * column's is `SurfDevActivity` (the rail it needs before it sheds a field).
 * The narrowest terminal serving both is their sum, and only a seam near
 * their ratio collects it.
 *
 * When 7:6 was picked those needs were 81 and 71, summing to 152. This was
 * `3fr`/`2fr` until 2026-08-10, which hands the feed 0.60 W against the 0.538
 * it needed, so the rail reached 71 columns only at 176 -- 24 columns wider
 * than the layout required and past the ~169 a laptop gets at the 17 pt
 * `__main__` forces, i.e. the full layout was unreachable at the app's own
 * font size.
 *
 * Both needs have since fallen -- the feed to 76 columns, the rail to 63 --
 * so the sum is 139 and 7:6 collects it at 142. Re-swept seam by seam over
 * the real screen at the current widget widths (table in
 * tests/screens/test_surf_screen.py): 3:2 -> 156, 5:3 -> 166, 7:5 -> 149,
 * 4:3 -> 145, 5:4 -> 140, 11:9 -> 139, 7:6 -> 142, 1:1 -> 152. 139 is the
 * floor now; 7:6 sits three columns above it, and those three columns buy
 * nothing a user sees, because FWA's 143 is what the app documents. Leave
 * the seam alone until one of the two needs moves again. */
SurfFeed {
    width: 7fr;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The right rail: SIGNALS above DEV ACTIVITY. No padding of its own -- both
 * children already pad, and doubling it here would cost the activity rows a
 * column on each side, which they pay for out of the anti-poisoning address
 * window.
 *
 * `overflow-y: auto` is the short-terminal guard, and the same answer the
 * bakery `SignalsPanel` above uses. The old layout gave the signals a fixed
 * ten-row band in the hero row, so all six detectors survived down to a
 * ~16-row terminal; sizing the rows to their content means the rail is only
 * as tall as the middle row, and below 36 rows its fifteen lines no longer
 * fit. Scrolling is the *affordance*: nothing is dropped, it is all still
 * reachable. The *advertisement* is `TALLER_HINT` on the title bar (see
 * screens/surf.py) -- a scrollbar in a gutter names nothing it took away,
 * and both halves of the rail can lose their own titles before it appears.
 * `scrollbar-size: 1 1` matches every other scrollable container in this
 * file; the Textual default is two cells wide. */
SurfScreen #surf-right-rail {
    width: 6fr;
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    scrollbar-size: 1 1;
}

/* padding stays `0 1` -- horizontal only.
 *
 * SurfSignals sizes to a title plus six detector rows. Vertical padding drops
 * the sixth row, and the sixth row is the BURN detector: the panel would still
 * look complete while one of the six advertised signals was simply absent.
 * That is the FWA coverage-badge bug (see the FWAHeroBox comment above) in a
 * different widget, and tests/test_surf_registration.py asserts all six reach
 * the compositor.
 *
 * `margin: 0 0 1 0` is the one blank row between the rail's two panels. They
 * sat flush and read as one block. A margin rather than a spacer `Static`:
 * nothing to compose, nothing to query, nothing for a screen test to have to
 * skip past -- and it collapses into the rail's scroll extent like any other
 * row, which is what keeps the `‹ taller` marker honest about it. It costs
 * the rail exactly one row, so the height at which that marker lights moved
 * 35 -> 36 and the height at which a real activity row is first lost moved
 * 33 -> 34: the marker still leads the loss by two rows. */
SurfSignals {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The rail's lower half. `1fr` so it absorbs the rail's spare rows, and
 * `min-height` because a `1fr` child cannot overflow its scroll container --
 * it shrinks. `SurfMarket` was `1fr` in this rail with no floor and shed one
 * row per terminal row from 35 down, reaching "title only" at 31 and a blank
 * rail at 30, with no scrollbar, no marker and no trace anywhere on screen.
 * The floor turns that silent thinning into an overflow, which is what makes
 * the scrollbar and the title bar's `‹ taller` fire on the same terminal row
 * the first line is lost on. 7 == `screens/surf.ACTIVITY_MIN_HEIGHT` (a title,
 * a spacer and five rows); the two numbers are pinned to each other by
 * tests/test_surf_registration.py, since CSS cannot read a Python constant. */
SurfDevActivity {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
    min-height: 7;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* `auto`: the market's seven rows and the NFT panel's eight are content, not
 * slack. The shared `#bottom-row { height: 1fr }` above used to hand them half
 * the screen's spare rows, which left roughly a fifth of the screen blank
 * between the last sale and the status bar. The row sizes to the taller of the
 * two panels, which is the NFT one, so the market rides along for free. */
SurfScreen #bottom-row {
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* Same 7:6 seam as the middle row, so the two rows read as one grid and the
 * columns line up down the screen. Neither of these two panels is what sets
 * the seam -- both are clean far below the measured width -- so they follow
 * the middle row rather than constraining it. */
SurfMarket {
    width: 7fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

SurfNft {
    width: 6fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The two scrolling panes. `SurfFeed` and `SurfDevActivity` each compose a
 * title `Static` plus a `RichLog` (see their DEFAULT_CSS), and the `RichLog`
 * is what must absorb the leftover height -- without `height: 1fr` it sizes to
 * its content and the panel grows or shrinks as posts arrive.
 *
 * No `DataTable` rule here on purpose: not one of the six surf widgets mounts
 * a `DataTable`. Feed and activity use `RichLog`; hero, signals, market and
 * NFT are `Static` rows only. A selector for a widget that is never mounted
 * matches nothing and reads, to the next person, as though it were load-
 * bearing.
 *
 * `padding` and `scrollbar-size` are deliberately *not* restated: the widget
 * DEFAULT_CSS owns them, this block already pads the containing widget, and
 * restating the padding here would double it. */
SurfFeed > RichLog,
SurfDevActivity > RichLog {
    height: 1fr;
}

/* ── Curator screen (THE LIST) ────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* Structure only, restating CuratorScreen.DEFAULT_CSS. An app stylesheet
 * always outranks DEFAULT_CSS in Textual, so anything this block leaves out is
 * not "inherited from the screen" -- it is whatever this file already says for
 * the shared ids (#title-bar, #middle-row, #separator, #bottom-row, lines
 * 12-130). Same reasoning, and same failure mode, as the FWA and surf blocks
 * above. The two copies are asserted to agree
 * (tests/test_curator_registration.py); edit both or neither.
 *
 * The id rules are scoped to `CuratorScreen` on purpose. `#hero-row` and
 * `#curator-right-rail` have no shared rule today, and an unscoped rule for
 * either would silently become law for the next dashboard that picks the same
 * id -- while the shared `#middle-row` / `#bottom-row` rules above ARE law for
 * ten other screens, so the curator overrides have to be scoped to beat them
 * here and nowhere else. The `Curator*` widget types stay unscoped: those
 * seven types are mounted by this screen and nothing else.
 *
 * Colour belongs in a Theme, not here: this stylesheet is shared by all ten
 * themes, so naming a theme-specific variable would raise a parse error under
 * the other nine. THE LIST gets no theme of its own in v1 (PRD §10) and reads
 * correctly under all ten, which is what the parametrised render test asserts.
 *
 * `#title-bar` and `#separator` are deliberately NOT restated here. The shared
 * rules already style both ids for every screen, and the curator screen's
 * measured worst-case title (WORST_CASE_TITLE_COLUMNS = 90) was swept with the
 * shared `padding: 0 2` in force. Restating them would give a shared rule a
 * second owner -- the surf block's documented asymmetry.
 *
 * No widget-internal class belongs here either (.curator-lb-*,
 * .curator-signals-*, .curator-spark-*, .curator-activity-title, .curator-cc-*,
 * .curator-cl-*, #curator-hero-boxes, CuratorHeroBox, #curator-hero-note): all
 * of them are owned by the seven widget modules' own DEFAULT_CSS, none of them
 * collides with a shared selector, and restating one would give that rule two
 * owners for no gain.
 */

/* `auto`, not a pinned height, and NO vertical padding. CuratorHero is a
 * height-8 widget: three height-7 boxes over the one-row EOA subtitle. A
 * single row of vertical padding here clips the subtitle -- or the boxes'
 * bottom border -- off the screen, which is the FWA hero coverage-badge bug
 * exactly, and against `auto` it would also silently pad the row back out. */
CuratorScreen #hero-row {
    height: auto;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The hero owns the whole top row: three boxes across the full width. */
CuratorHero {
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The only `1fr` row above the separator, so the rows a taller terminal adds
 * land in the leaderboard and the rail rather than in the tables below. */
CuratorScreen #middle-row {
    height: 1fr;
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The middle row's seam is 3:5, measured rather than chosen: CuratorLeaderboard
 * needs 48 content columns and CuratorSignals needs 84 against the widest real
 * wallet in the capture; with each slot's own `padding: 0 1` and the rail's
 * reserved scrollbar gutter that sums to 137, and only a seam near 50:87
 * collects it. Re-swept with the gutter in force
 * (tests/screens/test_curator_screen.py): 10:17, 7:12, 13:22 and 17:29 reach
 * 137; 3:5 and 4:7 reach 138; 5:8 costs 140; 1:2 costs 150; 1:1 costs 173.
 * Re-sweep before re-seaming, and only when one of those two needs moves. */
CuratorLeaderboard {
    width: 3fr;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The right rail: VOLUME sparklines above SIGNALS. No padding of its own --
 * both children already pad -- and no VERTICAL padding for the reason one step
 * further down than the hero's: the rail holds a *seven*-row signal panel
 * whose last row is YOU, and a fixed-height column loses its last row first.
 *
 * `overflow-y: auto` is the short-terminal guard; scrolling is the affordance
 * (nothing is unreachable) and the *advertisement* is `TALLER_HINT` on the
 * title bar, because a one-cell scrollbar names nothing it took away and
 * Textual paints it outside the rail's rectangle at very short heights.
 *
 * `scrollbar-gutter: stable` is load-bearing, not cosmetic (commit 98b8ae6).
 * Without it the scrollbar takes its column out of CuratorSignals -- the panel
 * that binds this layout's width -- only on terminals under 42 rows, so the
 * *width* requirement moved with the *height* and the pinned 138 was true at
 * 48 rows and one column short at 40. Reserving the gutter spends that column
 * at every height instead of at one of them. */
CuratorScreen #curator-right-rail {
    width: 5fr;
    height: 1fr;
    overflow-y: auto;
    scrollbar-gutter: stable;
    scrollbar-size: 1 1;
}

/* `margin: 0 0 1 0` is the one blank row between the rail's two panels; it
 * collapses into the rail's scroll extent like any other row, which keeps the
 * `‹ taller` marker honest about it. */
CuratorSparklines {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* Horizontal padding only. CuratorSignals sizes to a title plus seven detector
 * rows and vertical padding drops the seventh, which is YOU: the panel would
 * still look complete while one of the seven advertised rows was simply
 * absent. That is the FWA coverage-badge bug in a different widget. */
CuratorSignals {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* `1fr`, NOT `auto`, and it is the surf block's choice inverted on purpose.
 * Both of this row's slots are `height: auto`, and CuratorActivity's own
 * content is a full deposit window: given `auto` the row sizes to it, takes
 * 47 of the screen's rows, and starves `#middle-row` down to a single row --
 * SIGNALS and YOU then never reach the compositor at all, a screen scrollbar
 * appears and eats two columns, and the width sweep "passes" at 136 with the
 * whole rail gone. `1fr` splits the spare rows between the two rows instead,
 * which is the geometry every measurement in WP6 was taken against. */
CuratorScreen #bottom-row {
    height: 1fr;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The bottom row's seam is 5:3, also measured: CuratorActivity needs 77
 * columns and the wider of the two swap tables (CuratorClusters) needs 45, a
 * floor of 126 -- comfortably inside the middle row's 138, so this row follows
 * that seam rather than setting it. */
CuratorActivity {
    width: 5fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The `c` pair. Both are mounted and both are dispatched to on every refresh;
 * exactly one is visible at a time, so they share one slot and therefore share
 * one width. Identical geometry is what makes the swap invisible. */
CuratorClosestCalls {
    width: 3fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

CuratorClusters {
    width: 3fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE LIST's `y` view -- the reader's own standing.
 *
 * Two bodies live under one hero: `#curator-dashboard-body` is the game,
 * `#curator-wallet-body` is this. Exactly one is displayed, so both claim the
 * same `1fr` of the screen's remaining height and the hero above them never
 * moves when the reader presses `y`. A body sized `auto` here would resize the
 * hero on the toggle, which is the same class of mistake as restating
 * `#bottom-row { height: auto }` (see the note above it).
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */
CuratorScreen #curator-dashboard-body {
    height: 1fr;
    width: 100%;
}

CuratorScreen #curator-wallet-body {
    height: 1fr;
    width: 100%;
}

/* The ladder is the wider half: five columns of numbers against the standing
 * panel's four label/value lines. 3:2 measured, same method as #middle-row. */
CuratorScreen #wallet-top-row {
    height: 1fr;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

CuratorScreen #wallet-bottom-row {
    height: auto;
}

CuratorScreen #wallet-separator {
    color: $panel;
    height: 1;
}

CuratorScreen CuratorWalletLadder {
    width: 3fr;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 0 1;
}

CuratorScreen CuratorWalletStanding {
    width: 2fr;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 0 1;
}

CuratorScreen CuratorWalletNext {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    min-height: 3;
    padding: 0 1;
}
